    {
      "section_id": "trainingdatavisualization_06_detail_section",
      "section_order": 6,
      "html_id": null,
      "element": "section",
      "section_name": "Detail Section",
      "section_heading": "Good questions.",
      "heading_level": "h2",
      "eyebrow": "A little more clarity",
      "subheading": "",
      "description": "",
      "paragraphs": [
        "A little more clarity",
        "For Power BI, the free desktop version covers the whole program, so no upfront license cost. Tableau offers trial licenses that cover a training window. We'll confirm the exact setup checklist before day one.",
        "Yes, that's the most common pairing: Excel for the analysts' daily work, Power BI for what leadership sees. Adding Tableau too usually means extending the schedule rather than diluting all three.",
        "Wherever possible, yes, under NDA if required, because a dashboard built on your real numbers keeps getting used after the trainer leaves. Sanitised extracts work fine when live access isn't practical.",
        "Yes. If your team's daily work lives in Excel, the program can run entirely as advanced Excel training for employees: pivot tables, formulas, data cleanup and report formatting, with the BI tools left out or added later."
      ],
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "A little more clarity"
        },
        {
          "type": "h2",
          "text": "Good questions."
        },
        {
          "type": "summary",
          "text": "Do we need Power BI or Tableau licenses before the training?+"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "For Power BI, the free desktop version covers the whole program, so no upfront license cost. Tableau offers trial licenses that cover a training window. We'll confirm the exact setup checklist before day one."
        },
        {
          "type": "summary",
          "text": "Can one batch cover both Power BI and Excel?+"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Yes, that's the most common pairing: Excel for the analysts' daily work, Power BI for what leadership sees. Adding Tableau too usually means extending the schedule rather than diluting all three."
        },
        {
          "type": "summary",
          "text": "Will you work with our actual company data?+"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Wherever possible, yes, under NDA if required, because a dashboard built on your real numbers keeps getting used after the trainer leaves. Sanitised extracts work fine when live access isn't practical."
        },
        {
          "type": "summary",
          "text": "Do you run Excel-only training for employees?+"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Yes. If your team's daily work lives in Excel, the program can run entirely as advanced Excel training for employees: pivot tables, formulas, data cleanup and report formatting, with the BI tools left out or added later."
        }
      ],
